A||N||9||Monkeyman||Driving lights, please?||||1||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||05-30-2000||09:59 AM||||I'm about ready to add some driving lights to the Grape Ape.  I need 2 things, please.  First, I'd like to see any pics of your cars with driving lights installed.  I want to get an idea of what some look like installed and where you've put them.  Second, can someone tell me the "right" way to install them?  Relay or not?  Different kinds of mountings?  Etc?<P>Thanx everybuggy.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||05-30-2000||02:07 PM||||I have 75 watt projector type mounted behind the nose with the lens' sticking into the nose area. Mine are wired so the parking lights must be on to turn them on. It is controlled by a relay. There is also a by-pass switch inside so I can turn the lights off while the other lights are on. The set-up is very simular to how the newer cars have their driving lights set-up. I would always use a relay otherwise you will need a heavy duty switch to handle the current the lights draw. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FLVIEW.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000002||Monkeyman||05-30-2000||02:31 PM||||2 questions.<P>Are there any advantages or disadvantages to the different style lights?  I've seen a few Fieros with large round lights (take up most of the top-to-bottom space in the air intake?) and some with small lamps like you have, Oreif.<P>Let me see if I understand how a relay works.  The hot wire runs directly from the battery to the lamps except that it has a relay somewhere inbetween.  The only thing that the dash mounted switch does is "turn on" the relay to let the juice run through?  Is there no actual power running through the dash mounted switch??||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pontiaddict||05-30-2000||03:55 PM||||a relay uses a small amount of current flowing through the switch to turn it on and send a lot of current to something (lights for example) it has less current draw than a switch and it isn't gonna burn the switch out. It is the best way to wire high amp electrical pieces that need to be turned on/off with a switch.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula||05-30-2000||04:08 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/formulafront222.JPG">  <P>Mine use a relay and a switch too.  But I can turn them on anytime when the car is running.  I have them so that they cant be turned on while the car is off for battery charging reasons  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||87ECO5||05-30-2000||04:37 PM||||Has anyone with the Formula/'87-'88 nose ever thought of giving it a C4 Corvette touch:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/formulamod1.jpg"> <P>The grooves on the inner sides of the parking lights might look real good cut out with a set of rectangular lights stuffed in them.  Yes, it would require some modification.  But, it would also look more natural.<P>(Formula - thanks for the use of your car to demonstrate)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula||05-30-2000||04:49 PM||||That looks cool.  If I had an extra nose (lol) then I would do it.  (the nose thing sounds funny).<P>One of my friends told me to cut the rear deflectors out and run my exhaust through there, but There would be a small heat problem in my trunk.<P>have you measured a vettes lights to see if they would fit in our rectangular area.  If so I bet Tom could make an easy to do mod for it!||||reg||1
Z||000007||2M4 Dale||05-31-2000||12:26 AM||||I have noticed that the front of my 87 coupe looks amazingly like the front of a C4 Vette. Sounds like a really cool idea Scott. You do it FIRST and if it works out I'll try it!!!...lol||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||06-02-2000||10:26 AM||||I'd still like to see some more pics.  I remember someone with large round ones set in their ?intake? and thought there was another one with them in place of the running lights?||||reg||1
